;;; A pair contradiction's core names only its positive side, where that side
;;; is a fact the program states — regression
;;; `pair-core-root-given` (`corpus/regressions.toml`).
;;;
;;;
;;; ── What this program is ───────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; One candidate, `(q a)`, which the hrule proposes. Committed, it derives
;;; `(not (p a))` by `neg`, and the program states `(p a)`: a pair
;;; contradiction whose positive side is root's and whose negative side the
;;; commitment derives.
;;;
;;; ── The answer, by hand ────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; R is {∅}: root is alive, and {(q a)} is dead, N's one member. The pair
;;; rests on both its sides: `(p a)`, stated, and `(not (p a))`, which `neg`
;;; derives from `(q a)`. So the core is `(p a) (q a)`, which names the
;;; commitment, and from it, with the program's rules, saturation derives
;;; the pair. `(p a)` alone is root's, and root is alive: no refutation.
;;;
;;; ── What the engine answered, and what it answers ──────────────────────
;;;
;;; At `cdcc3994` (`ein solve FILE --dump-answer DIR`, release and debug): R
;;; as above, exit 0; and N's core `(p a)` alone, refuted by `(given)` and
;;; `neg`, in `--dump-answer`'s `nogoods.json`, `--dump-lattice`'s
;;; `nodes.json`, `--events`' `enter` line, `--trace` and `ein oracle --dump`.
;;; The core search explained each contradiction by its witness alone, and a
;;; pair's witness is its positive side
;;; (`contradiction::Contradiction::witness`). Today it explains a
;;; contradiction's sides together, and N's core is `(p a) (q a)`, in either
;;; build.

(relation p T :why "p holds of {?3}") (relation q T :why "q holds of {?3}")

(p a)

(hrule g () :match (p ?x) :assert (q ?x) :why "p holds of {?x}: guess that q holds of {?x} too")

(rule neg () :match (q ?x) :assert (not (p ?x)) :why "q holds of {?x}, so p does not hold of {?x}")
